{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/588563-post-your-ideas-about-different-setting-takes-plot-hooks","id":588563,"title":"Post your ideas about different setting takes/plot hooks.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-12T16:52:48-07:00","id":588563,"title":"Post your ideas about different setting takes/plot hooks.","content":"VERY IMPORTANT DISCLAIMER: please share ideas for different ways to think about and tell stories, not the idea that your stories are better than others simply and solely because they are being told by you. I assume no bad faith on anyone's part here, but please remain civil.<br /> <br /> The cautionary tale about changelings reaching Wyrd 10, losing all Clarity and becoming a True Fae was started by a particularly cunning member of the Scarecrow Ministry, perhaps a Minister himself. It <b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">is</span> </b>possible to become one of the Others, but accumulating mystical power/narrative prominence is not enough. You have to stand before them and offer a Tithe, which can be anything, as long as it was somehow tied to an act that decisively separates you from all puny mortals. This secret is kept very well guarded indeed, even by Autumn and Winter Court standards.<br /> <br /> Billy Birch is not the first entity in the Hedge to acquire sapience. The first thinking being of the Hedge is dead but dreaming, and Billy wrote about that being on a piece of wood somewhere very well hidden to expunge his memory and prevent others from disturbing the First Thorn. Any Gentry with even a crumb of knowledge about the First Thorn refuses to reveal all they know, even when surrounded by a circle of hand forged iron spears. Why is this so? Is the First Thorn so unspeakably diabolical and powerful that even the Gentry don't want anyone trying to wake it up? Is the First Thorn somehow capable of banishing the Others from Earth forever? What would be the fae price of such an eternal protection?<br /> <br /> The Wyrd can be described as an Author. The duty of any author is to tell a compelling story, and to grab attention, there must be conflict. Changelings often think of themselves as survivors who were scarred by their time with their Keepers, and that is completely understandable. But the truth is less grim, depending on your point of view: changelings are more than just chess pieces on the hands of a bored narrative force. They are meant to reach their own version of Wyrd Transcendence, an Ending that echoes in many stories for eons to come. They no longer need to play the Game Of Immortals. They won, and those who follow in their steps will know how to play better than those before. The Gentry also play the Game Of Immortals, of course, but it would surprise many of them to look into the eye of the Wyrd for longer than necessary to pledge &quot;I AM&quot;. What awaits <b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">them</span></b> at the end of Wyrd Transcendence can only be described as the ultimate denial of Agency, a state of all encompassing powerlessness that is revealed bit by bit. What they partially see each time makes the very foundations of Arcadia tremble in uncertainty and self-contemplation for but a moment. The Wyrd offers them chances to escape that doom of all dooms, but the Wyrd being what it is, the price is superlative: the Lords and Ladies have to reject their cosmic privilege of knowing from the very start the why of their existence. It requires them to, if not be human, at least be infused with some kind of flaw outside of the Pledge Of Imperfection. The Gentry have to go through Durances of their own, filled with hardships as painful for them as the ones in a mortal's Durance. In short, they must CHANGE. None have made it all the way so far, and the Wyrd is not holding its breath. It is patient beyond comprehension, and wise enough to know the truth: only writing stories where all the antagonists can never receive the slightest opportunity to be something else makes it a bad Author.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":4443,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","user":"GibberingEloquence","date":". 07-12-2015, 08:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-14T02:44:14-07:00","id":590063,"title":"","content":"Oldest Thorn and Wyrd sound interesting. Here are mine:<br /> <br /> ''We call Gentry Cousins. Do you wonder why is that? That's how some of them see us. We aren't born of pure Wyrd like them, but similar to Arcadia, in us Wyrd is stronger than most of world. So some of Fair Folk come out and seek their slaves, believing them family. they try to take them back, to '' develop their potential.'''One changeling I knew managed to reclaim his life. He could hide what happened to him. Then, one day, his Keeper showed up on family meeting, and poor guy admited it all to his family. They tried to banish fiend, but strange Contract kicked in, and it was bound to family, unable to harm them, but they too couldn't raise hand against it. They say that Keeper tried to bond with changeling. '' How did that thing balance power and consequences of becoming Charlatan?<br /> <br /> Each True Fae is ruler in their own right. No True Fae bows to another, and they war because of it. They take humans so they would have someone to rule over. But strange things are happening in Faery. True Fae are coming together and uniting, forming hierarchy. Hedge Beasts and hobs are leaving Hedge and becoming vassals to Gentry. We call them Gentry, Throng, Lords and Ladies, names old as Lost are. Noble names. Could that mean that they have rulers of their own? And that they are coming? And could Lost stand against Kings and Queens?<br /> <br /> '' Creation. That is what we have and they don't. But they can copy, and they steal. And they grow stronger as time passes, as tales are forgotten and cold iron lost.'' Some of them may struck special Contracts, perform rites for Wyrd. All to gain this ability? But is it worth price?<br /> <br /> Gentry started iron age. They pulled human kind forth, and developed them? Why, wouldn't it weaken them. No. Tales are lost. People don't bother with supernatural. Other forces cannot help them. Iron is made hot little by little, and more and more we lose.<br /> <br /> '' So you learnt about Fiend's game. Does it interest what it is, what price that they pay with our lives? It frees them. They go to upper Arcadia, heeaven of their own. free of flaws, of limitations and of iron.'' They become saints and gods of Kindly Ones, able to create and to harm iron. What would happen if one of those came here?<br /> <br /> All True Fae, whole of Arcadia, is aspect of one. Spirit of their wretched universe. When Last Feud end, they will be whole. And Wyrd will reclaim it's child-one of numerous others.<br /> <br /> '' All comes with price. Here is what some say. True Fae could keep it's power on earth. It could come whole, with every Title and domain, and it could destroy world. But there needs to be conflict.''For if it comes whole, one hit and it could be completely destroyed. All Titles gone, no reserves, forever unmade.<br /> <br /> There is thing hidden out there. Machine that dreams of becoming magic. It attempts to twist changelings, to serve it as it's own did. It tried to take them once. But it backfired. All comes from wyrd, and machine wasn't exception. Wyrd, one that balances chaos and order both, sent forth it's spawn. Though machine could manipulate nature, it was within it's own laws. Strangers had different bargains, and nature moved past those laws to aid them. they tricked machine-first few died, and machine sent it's servants in Hedge. From that day, there was law of machine that even demons listened-if you see fae, <b>run</b>.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6005-coolcat12345","name":"coolcat12345","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}